<p>Rosser designed Mandan's first depot in April 1879. The one-story, gable-roofed building measured 24 by 70 feet and had a 12-foot-wide platform on all sides.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/northern-pacific-first-passenger-depot/485-np-depot-and-freighthouse-locations.jpg" alt="Line sketch showing Mandan's depot and freight-house locations">
    <figcaption>Excerpt from William Brown's 1910 line sketch of Mandan</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In 1892, a flagpole from {% place "fort-abraham-lincoln" %}Fort Abraham Lincoln{% endplace %} was installed in front of the building. After the {% place "northern-pacific-queen-anne-depot" %}Queen Anne depot{% endplace %} opened three blocks west, the original depot was enlarged and used exclusively as a freight house.</p>

<p>The building was sold and moved in 1913 to make way for a four-story brick warehouse beside the main track. The Missouri Valley Grocery and Produce Company built the warehouse, which the John Iverson Company later purchased and a bank subsequently occupied.</p>

<p>The wooden depot was moved to the northeast corner of First Street and Third Avenue Northeast and converted into a duplex. When it was demolished around 1980, its bay window was salvaged and given to the State Historical Society, which reportedly placed it in storage.</p>

